Q: C# compiler issues a compile error when implementing == and != operators record definitions A: <p>To correct a bug in <a href="https://github.com/AdamWhiteHat/BigDecimal" rel="nofollow noreferrer">BigDecimal</a> struct linked to an equality issue and defined as</p>
<pre><code>namespace ExtendedNumerics
{
    public readonly record struct BigDecimal 
        : IComparable
        , IComparable&lt;BigDecimal&gt;
        , IComparable&lt;Int32&gt;
        , IComparable&lt;Int32?&gt;
        , IComparable&lt;Decimal&gt;
        , IComparable&lt;Double&gt;
        , IComparable&lt;Single&gt;
    {
</code></pre>
<p>I defined only following member's function</p>
<pre><code>public bool Equals(BigDecimal other)
{
    if (!Sign.Equals(other.Sign)) return false;
    if (Exponent.Equals(other.Exponent)) return Mantissa.Equals(other.Mantissa);
    return GetWholePart().Equals(other.GetWholePart());
} 
</code></pre>
<p>Caution: <code>BigDecimal</code> is a <code>record struct</code> and not a <code>record</code> or <code>record class</code>. In my case (= for struct) compiler doesn't accept <code>virtual</code> key.</p>
<p>In <code>BigDecimal</code>, <code>Equals</code> function is necessary because BigDecimal <code>9.10^1</code> is equal to <code>90.10^0</code> !</p>

Q: Spring 4 RestTestClient - unable to send Multipart data A: <p>Your diagnosis is correct
this is a known limitation of <code>RestTestClient</code> when using <code>MOCK</code> web environment.</p>
<p>When you bind to the application context via <code>bindToApplicationContext</code>, <code>RestTestClient</code> uses <code>MockMvc</code> under the hood, routing requests through <code>MockMvcClientHttpRequest</code>.
The problem is that <code>MockMvc</code>'s multipart handling <strong>requires</strong> a <code>MockMultipartHttpServletRequestBuilder</code> a completely separate builder that <code>MockMvcClientHttpRequest</code> never uses. So even though the bytes are physically present in the request body (as your logs confirm), the servlet layer never parses them as multipart parts.</p>
<p>This isn't a bug
it's a consequence of <code>MOCK</code> mode intercepting requests before they reach a real servlet container. Postman works fine because it hits a real server with an actual multipart resolver in the chain.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Option 1 — Use a real servlet container (recommended if you want <code>RestTestClient</code>)</h2>
<p>Switch to <code>RANDOM_PORT</code> and let Spring Boot auto-configure <code>RestTestClient</code> against it:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
public class TestRestClientParts {

    @Autowired
    private RestTestClient client;

    @Test
    void sendMultiPart() {
        MultiValueMap&lt;String, Object&gt; body = new LinkedMultiValueMap&lt;&gt;();
        body.add(&quot;value&quot;, &quot;test&quot;);

        client.post()
                .uri(&quot;/api/multipart&quot;)
                .contentType(MediaType.MULTIPART_FORM_DATA)
                .body(body)
                .exchangeSuccessfully()
                .expectBody().json(&quot;{\&quot;value\&quot;: \&quot;test\&quot;}&quot;);
    }
}
\```

Real Tomcat/Jetty handles multipart parsing correctly here. The tradeoff is slightly slower test startup compared to `MOCK`.

---

## Option 2 — Stay with `MOCK` but use `MockMvc` directly

`MockMvc` has dedicated multipart support via its own DSL — you just can't get to it through `RestTestClient`:
```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.MOCK)
@AutoConfigureMockMvc
public class TestRestClientParts {

    @Autowired
    private MockMvc mockMvc;

    @Test
    void sendMultiPart() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(
                multipart(&quot;/api/multipart&quot;)
                        .param(&quot;value&quot;, &quot;test&quot;)
        )
        .andExpect(status().isOk())
        .andExpect(jsonPath(&quot;$.value&quot;).value(&quot;test&quot;));
    }
}
\```

For binary file parts swap `.param()` for `.file(new MockMultipartFile(&quot;file&quot;, &quot;test.txt&quot;, &quot;text/plain&quot;, bytes))`.

---

`RestTestClient` + `MOCK` + multipart is a dead end. 
Pick one:
- Want `RestTestClient` fluent API → use `RANDOM_PORT`
- Want to stay in `MOCK` → use `MockMvc` with `multipart()`
